​​Americans buy and lease millions of vehicles every year - some of which leave the factory with persistent problems.

If you’ve ended up with one of these faulty cars, you know how exhausting the aftermath can be. Between repeated trips to the repair facility, mounting expenses, and lost time, you can’t help but wonder if you’ll ever get the reliable vehicle you paid for.

Defective vehicles (commonly referred to as lemons) can result from various problems, such as faulty parts, subpar materials, or errors during assembly. Fortunately, consumer protection laws exist to safeguard your rights and provide compensation for the inconvenience and financial burden caused by a lemon.

## **Compensation for lemon vehicles typically comes in two forms:

A lemon law buyback or a regular car refund. Join the [CLINE APC team](/) as we break down both options to help you determine which solution works best for your situation.

## What is a Lemon Law Buyback?

A lemon law buyback refers to a vehicle that has been officially classified as a lemon and is subsequently repurchased by the manufacturer or dealer.

In most cases, a [lemon law buyback](/blog/the-components-of-a-lemon-law-refund-in-california) includes the total purchase price of the vehicle, taxes, registration fees, and any financing charges you incurred when buying or leasing the car. Additionally, manufacturers are typically allowed to deduct a reasonable usage fee to account for the time and mileage you were able to use the vehicle without experiencing significant problems.

Under California’s [Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act](https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&amp&division=3.&amp&title=1.7.&amp&part=4.&amp&chapter=1.&amp&article=3.#:~:text=The%20amount%20directly%20attributable%20to,having%20as%20its%20numerator%20the), the usage fee is calculated using the following formula:

Usage Fee = (Miles Driven Before First Repair Attempt/120,000) x Purchase Price of the Vehicle

To make sure the calculation is accurate and prevent the manufacturer from overcharging for usage, it’s best to consult a California lemon law attorney. They can review the details of your case, verify the math, and advocate for your full and fair refund.

## What Is a Regular Refund?

A regular car refund is a simpler option offered by a manufacturer or dealer. It typically happens outside the California lemon law framework and is presented as a goodwill gesture or negotiated settlement.

Regular vehicle refunds may cover:

- The purchase price, adjusted based on depreciation.

- Sales tax, registration fees, and a few other upfront costs.

However, standard refunds may not include out-of-pocket expenses, such as towing fees, rental car expenses, or any legal costs incurred while dealing with the defective vehicle. If you financed the car, there’s no guarantee the refund will cover your remaining loan balance, potentially leaving you responsible for the difference.

Regular car refunds favor the manufacturer or dealer because they are less expensive and provide fewer obligations. Manufacturers may offer this option as a way to avoid lemon law claims, hoping consumers will accept a quick settlement rather than pursuing the comprehensive compensation available under lemon law.

## Buyback vs Refunds: Key Differences

With the basics covered, let’s discuss the biggest differences between these two options. Spoiler: One is much better for you as the consumer.

## Who Initiates the Process

**Lemon Law Buyback**: You (and your lemon law attorney) are in control. You invoke the lemon law, and the manufacturer must comply if your lemon law claim qualifies.

**Regular Refund**: The dealer or manufacturer offers this as a courtesy or settlement. You might have to negotiate heavily to get it - and it might not be sufficient to cover all your losses.

## What’s Covered

**Lemon Law Buyback**: Comprehensive coverage, including the purchase price, incidental expenses, loan payoff, attorney fees, court costs, etc.

**Regular Refund**: Limited to the purchase price and some applicable taxes. Incidental costs are mostly ignored.

## Legal Backing

**Lemon Law Buyback**: This is backed by state law. If the manufacturer doesn’t comply, you are legally entitled to enforce your rights.

**Regular Refund**: There’s no legal obligation here - it’s more of a goodwill gesture. If the manufacturer decides to back out, you’re left with little recourse.

## What About Replacement Vehicles?

Sometimes, manufacturers or dealers may offer a replacement vehicle (of the same value) instead of a refund for your defective car. The terms and benefits depend on whether the replacement is offered under lemon law or as part of a regular trade-in arrangement.

## Lemon Law Replacement

You may have the option to receive a replacement vehicle instead of a refund for your lemon vehicle. The replacement vehicle must be of similar value, make, and features to your original car.

For instance, if you purchased a mid-level sedan, the manufacturer cannot offer you a lower-tier or base model as a replacement. They must also cover taxes, registration fees, and any costs associated with the replacement process.

## Regular Trade-In

Dealers may suggest trading in your defective vehicle for another model from their inventory. Be sure to tread carefully. They may use this as an opportunity to upsell you into a pricier model or stick you with unfavorable financing terms. Always compare the trade-in value with market rates and review the contract carefully before agreeing to it.

## Is Replacement the Right Choice?

A replacement vehicle can be a good option if you’re still interested in driving a car from the same manufacturer and trust their product lineup. However, the replacement vehicle should meet your needs, and confirm that any additional costs, such as mileage offsets or dealer fees, are fair and transparent.

Additionally, manufacturers must agree to the terms of a replacement (as opposed to a buyback) under California lemon law.

## How Do You Know If You Qualify for a Lemon Law Buyback in California?

In California, a vehicle may qualify for a lemon law buyback if it has a significant defect that affects its use, safety, or value and cannot be repaired after a reasonable number of attempts. Generally, the defect must occur within the manufacturer’s warranty period.

California’s lemon law outlines these specific criteria:

- The defect was reported to the manufacturer within the terms of the original warranty.

- The defect persists despite repair attempts and is not due to misuse or neglect on the part of the vehicle owner.

- The car must have undergone **two or more repair attempts**for a serious safety issue or**four or more repair attempts** for a general defect.

- The lemon law buyback vehicle has been out of service for a total of** 30 or more days** due to repairs.

Under recent updates ([AB 1755](https://calmatters.digitaldemocracy.org/bills/ca_202320240ab1755?slug=CA_202320240AB1755)), lemon law claims must also be filed **within one year**after the warranty expires and**no later than six years** from the vehicle’s original delivery date.

Consumers whose vehicles don’t meet these criteria for a lemon law buyback in California may need to consider pursuing a regular refund instead. Although refunds are less inclusive, they can still provide some compensation when lemon law protections aren’t an option.

## How to Start a Lemon Law Buyback Claim

If you believe your vehicle qualifies for a California lemon law buyback, here’s what you’ll need to do to build a successful lemon law claim.

## Gather Documentation

Documentation is your strongest asset in a lemon law case. Collect all [repair orders, receipts, and invoices](/blog/what-records-are-needed-in-california-lemon-law-cases) from the dealership or service center. These should detail every attempt to fix the issue, including:

- Dates

- Descriptions of the defect

- Whether the problem was resolved

Furthermore, retain your purchase agreement, warranty information, and records of communication with the dealer or manufacturer. These documents help establish the terms of the sale, the manufacturer’s obligations, and your efforts to resolve the issue.

## Request Reimbursement or Replacement

Before officially filing a lemon law claim, it’s worth reaching out to the dealership or manufacturer directly to discuss your situation. Politely explain the recurring issues with your vehicle and why you believe it is a lemon.

If the manufacturer or car dealer is cooperative, they may offer to replace the vehicle or issue a refund voluntarily. Be cautious - make sure any offer fully compensates you for your expenses, including the car’s purchase price, taxes, fees, and other eligible costs.

On the contrary, if the dealership or manufacturer refuses to address the issue or offers less than what you’re entitled to, you’ll need to escalate your claim.

## Reach Out to a California Lemon Law Attorney

While you can technically file a claim on your own, the lemon law process can be complex and time-consuming- especially if the manufacturer resists.

A competent California lemon law lawyer can take the burden off your shoulders by handling all aspects of your claim. That includes filing paperwork, gathering evidence, and negotiating with the manufacturer on your behalf.

Additionally, many of these attorneys work on a [contingency basis](/blog/how-lemon-law-attorneys-work-on-a-contingency-fee-agreement), meaning you don’t pay any legal fees unless they successfully resolve your case. This makes their services more affordable and makes sure they’re motivated to secure the best possible outcome on your behalf.

## Final Thoughts

Lemon law buybacks provide stronger protections, greater financial recovery, and a more comprehensive resolution if your car qualifies. For vehicles that don’t meet lemon law criteria, a regular car refund can still offer partial compensation, though it may leave you covering additional costs.

